  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 PM Monday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Much of the upcoming workweek will be unseasonably \nwarm and dry with record highs possible on Wednesday and perhaps \nThursday. \n\nAn unseasonably strong upper level ridge centered over TX on Tuesday \nwill slowly build east during the work week, become centered over \nthe Southeast on Thursday and then begin to shift off the coast on \nFriday. The ridge across the southern CONUS will be noted by 500 mb \nheights of around 593-594dm during much of the week with the main \ncore of the westerlies anticyclonically riding atop the ridge in the \nMO and OH Valleys into the Northeast. A back door front is expected \nto drop into the mid-Atlantic late Wednesday into Thursday but \nguidance has increasingly suggested it will remain to our north \nacross WV and VA. Morning low level thickness values will warm from \n1380s this morning, the 1390s on Tuesday morning, the 1400s on \nWednesday and Thursday morning. These thickness values will support \nhighs in the mid to upper 80s which will easily threaten record \nhighs on Wednesday and possibly Thursday despite a little more cloud \ncover. These highs are also about 12 to 19 degrees about average!\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A strong cold front will sweep across the area \nFriday with a significant cooldown for the weekend and the \npotential for some patchy frost. \n\nA strong northern stream trough will move across the Great Lakes on \nFriday and off the New England Coast on Saturday. This feature will \ndrive a strong cold front across the Carolinas on Friday with the \nfront moving off the coast on Friday night. Deep layer moisture \nrecovers a bit ahead of the front with PW values reaching 125 to \n150% of normal but the best forcing for ascent and instability will \nbe located north of our area so precipitation chances are across \ncentral NC will be limited. \n\nLow level thickness values crash Friday night into Saturday and \nremain below average through the weekend. By Sunday morning \nthickness values will fall into the 1320s or 1330s as Canadian high \npressure builds into the region. Highs on Saturday and Sunday should \nrange in the lower to mid 60s which is about 6 to 10 degrees \nbelow average. Lows on Sunday and Monday morning will range in \nthe upper 30s to lower 40s. These lows are about 3 to 8 degrees \nbelow average. A few of the typically colder locations may reach\nthe mid 30s and experience some patchy frost.",
